e job  is a test tube and it's getting filled  with water  so we can talk about the remaining size  of a job that's the amount of work left  and we can also talk about the age of  the job that's the amount of work that's  been done so far these are also measured  in units of time  so we have arrivals to the system uh  jobs arriving in online fashion so  here's one job that's small here's a  bigger job and the arrivals happen  according to some stochastic process  so there's some arrival rate lambda and  jobs arrive at this average rate at  completely random times formerly a  possible process  there's some job size distribution from  which each job size is independently  drawn and this gives the system a  certain load  which is the average arrival rate times  the average job size and you can think  of load as the fraction of time the  server has to be busy in order to keep  up with all these arrivals in order to  get all the jobs done  and in order for the system to be stable  load has to be less than one so that's  what we're going to assume  beyond assuming that load is less than  one we won't make any further  assumptions uh that let me say the job  size distribution  this is important because in practice  different types of applications have  very different looking job size  distributions  okay so  that's the sort of stochastic queuing  model we're talk we're working with so  let's continue the story of maybe this  job at the server eventually its age  reaches its size this job is now  complete and so it exits the system  the total amount of time the job spent  in the system between its arrival and  departure is called the jobs response  time  and so this is what i mean when i say  delay for the rest of this talk delay is  a synonym for response time of jobs  okay so this job has exited the system  and we now have to decide what job gets  to go into the service next this is the  domain of the scheduling policy which  decides at every moment in time which  job to 