oping uh sort of concurrent  lines of work in both the worst case and  stochastic settings  okay so this is the noise model we're  going to be working with beta alpha  bounded noises  that come from some joint distribution  but we're not going to make any  assumptions about what that distribution  is and we're going to try and schedule  without actually knowing the  distribution  so in this setting  what's a reasonable goal in terms of  mean response time and so if we look at  the algorithms with predictions  literature there are a few kind of  common goals uh that we see when people  design algorithms for predictions  so for example consistency is one common  goal which is about having good  performance when the predictions are  very good  so formally we're going to call a policy  c consistent for some constant c  uh if that policy ps approximation ratio  namely its mean response time ratio  relative to srpt  converges to c or less as alpha and beta  converge to 1.  so that's consistency it's all about  good performance under low noise what if  there's tons of noise what if the  predictions are almost useless so a goal  that we'd aim for in that setting is  robustness so formally we'll define our  robustness as saying p is r robust if  it's approximation ratio is always  at most r for all alpha and beta  unfortunately it turns out our  robustness at least for constant r isn't  achievable in our setting  and so  and so uh so we can't hope for  robustness okay so so far we've talked  about the case of low noise and of like  really high noise what if we have medium  noise uh you know what's a goal we might  aim for it uh there especially given  that we can't get robustness so  um  so this is something that people often  try to do in the algorithms with  predictions literature but it doesn't  have a sort of common name so  we call this a concept graceful  degradation or in this talk just  graceful for short so we call a policy g  graceful  if its approximation ratio is