olicies  and one thing we did to help ourselves  out was to narrow our focus to subclass  of scheduling policies still a very  broad class of scheduling policies but  something that was a bit easier to think  about  and and for which some queuing theory  existed to kind of help us evaluate  scheduling policies and i'll get to a  bit of that later so the design space we  ended up looking at was something called  uh scheduling policies that can be  described as rank functions  so  remember that a job's age is the amount  of time it's been in service so far  it turns out that lots of policies in  the scheduling literature can be  described as a job having some priority  which we'll call a rank  that varies as a function of age  so you know so we're going to say that  rank is some numerical priority lower  number means better priority the  server's always going to serve whichever  job has the lowest rank  and this rank can vary as a function of  a job's age and also perhaps of its  static characteristics in our case  that's going to mean size and estimated  size as an example srpt  fits into this framework it has the rank  function s minus a that is a job's rank  is its remaining size and so srpt always  serves the job of least remaining size  now of course we're going to try and  design a rank function that doesn't use  s right because we don't have access to  the true sizes so if we don't have s but  we do have z what might we do instead of  srpt well the obvious idea is well z is  going to be close enough to s so let's  just use z instead so this is a policy  that we call naive it's the naive  translation of srpt  into the size estimate setting  okay and s and naive of course also has  a rank function it's just z minus a  and so in this work we're going to be  focusing on the design space  of policies that can be described as  rank functions and so when we're trying  to design a policy we're really asking  the question what's the right rank  function  so let's start 