es linearly down and  ends at zero by the time age equals size  s  um and so  what this means is that we can't  actually implement scale without knowing  a job's true size so you wouldn't really  use the scale policy in practice but  it's helpful for analysis because we can  compare radical to scale and then  compare scale to srpt  okay so how are we going to do these  comparisons so  uh this is where the second sentence of  this talk comes in which was that i'm in  the last year of my phd so the thesis  i'm writing is all about how do we  analyze scheduling policies in  stochastic queuing systems  and my thesis is all about two new tools  that i've developed  along with my collaborators including  isaac grossof and these new tools help  us analyze policies like radical scale  and srbt and in fact i'm really excited  about this paper in particular because  for the first time we had to use both  tools  so i'm going to tell you a little bit  about each of them so the first tool is  soap which we use to compare radical to  scale  and the second tool is called wine which  we use to compare scale to srbt so i'm  going to say just a few words about soak  and then i'll go a bit more depth into  wine and the reason i'm going to focus a  bit more on wine today is because wine  is actually potentially useful even for  worst case scheduling questions and so i  think that's a really important one to  share with the itcs community  okay so let's start with how we compare  radical to scale using this tool soap  so soap stands for schedule ordered by  hbase priority and what soap does is it  takes  basically any stochastic arrival process  and any rank function  and yields the response time  distribution it's a sort of complicated  formula the  formula for mean response time is a  little bit simpler but still pretty  complicated um  and so i'm going to leave defer the  details to the paper but the basic  version is that  because the radical and scale rank  functions are close enough to each other  and in particular because they both have  initial rank z  and never exceed that initial xe  the policies are close enough that soap  yields similar enough mean response  times for them  so that's how we do that first  comparison let's now talk about  comparing scale to srbt  using wine so wine stands for work  integral number equality and so unlike  soap which directly gave us response  time wine  is a way to get the number of jobs in  the system but this is fine because  there's a famous result in queuing  theory called little's law which says  that the mean response time is directly  proportional to the mean number of jobs  for any fixed arrival rate  and so  what and so the way wine gets us the  number of jobs in the system  is via this other quantity called our  work which i'll define soon and so what  mine does is it reduces the problem of  analyzing mean response time to the  problem of analyzing artwork or in our  case comparing scales artwork to srpt's  artwork so  in the rest of my talk i'm going to  answer some questions about wine first  what is our work second how does wine  give us n the number of jobs from our  work and then finally how do we use this  to bound scales mean response time  so let's start by defining our work and  before i define artwork let me just  define work without the r so when  queuing theorists talk about work they  just mean the  the total remaining size of all jobs in  the system  our work is going to be a filtered out  version of this total work so let's let  r be some  threshold  our work is the total remaining size of  all jobs  where i'm only counting the jobs that  are r and smaller in terms of their  remaining size so i like to visualize  our work as follows i've got some  threshold r glasses let me align the  jobs by remaining size and so when i put  on these glasses they're going to hide  the jobs that have remaining size above  threshold are  if i look at all the jobs that i can see  through the glasse