licy you could actually  do because you have to know a job's true  size in order to scale linearly down to  it  but if you were in a system where you  had uh not just an initial size estimate  but a  dynamically updating remaining size  estimate  then you probably couldn't do exactly  scale but you might have a sort of noisy  version of scale that you could do  and this lemma will also work if you're  doing some sort of noisy version of  scale  so that's what i wanted to say about  wine in summary what wine does is it  reduces the mean response time  comparison to an artwork comparison  and we showed that the artwork amounts  were close enough between scale and srbt  that they have similar enough mean  response times  okay let's summarize what we talked  about today so we discussed the problem  of minimizing mean response time when we  only have access to noisy size estimates  and this is a difficult problem because  the natural policies we'd think of end  up performing badly for example the  naive translation of srpt into size  estimate world ends up not even being  consistent so it has poor performance  even when the noise is very low  so to solve this problem we came up with  a new policy which has provably good  performance specifically we showed it as  one consistent so perfect consistency  and 3.5 graceful  and we proved this result using a  creative cocktail of two recently  developed uh tools from cueing theory  and so i'm excited about this general  direction  of algorithms with predictions uh where  we kind of consider stochastic versions  of the problems and i'm interested in  developing tools uh theoretical tools  for analyzing these stochastic problems  that go beyond just scheduling and  queuing so if you're curious to talk  about algorithms with predictions or you  have any questions about this talk feel  free to get in touch i'd be delighted to  hear from you

