oping uh sort of concurrent  lines of work in both the worst case and  stochastic settings  okay so this is the noise model we're  going to be working with beta alpha  bounded noises  that come from some joint distribution  but we're not going to make any  assumptions about what that distribution  is and we're going to try and schedule  without actually knowing the  distribution  so in this setting  what's a reasonable goal in terms of  mean response time and so if we look at  the algorithms with predictions  literature there are a few kind of  common goals uh that we see when people  design algorithms for predictions  so for example consistency is one common  goal which is about having good  performance when the predictions are  very good  so formally we're going to call a policy  c consistent for some constant c  uh if that policy ps approximation ratio  namely its mean response time ratio  relative to srpt  converges to c or less as alpha and beta  converge to 1.  so that's consistency it's all about  good performance under low noise what if  there's tons of noise what if the  predictions are almost useless so a goal  that we'd aim for in that setting is  robustness so formally we'll define our  robustness as saying p is r robust if  it's approximation ratio is always  at most r for all alpha and beta  unfortunately it turns out our  robustness at least for constant r isn't  achievable in our setting  and so  and so uh so we can't hope for  robustness okay so so far we've talked  about the case of low noise and of like  really high noise what if we have medium  noise uh you know what's a goal we might  aim for it uh there especially given  that we can't get robustness so  um  so this is something that people often  try to do in the algorithms with  predictions literature but it doesn't  have a sort of common name so  we call this a concept graceful  degradation or in this talk just  graceful for short so we call a policy g  graceful  if its approximation ratio is bounded by  g times some measure of the prediction  quality  and in our setting the natural measure  of quality turns out to be this ratio  alpha over beta it's kind of the  multiplicative size window or uh in  which the size estimate lives  okay um and  and so i would call this concept of g  gracefulness it's not a new thing it's a  slightly newish that we're sort of  calling it out because i think um it  definitely doesn't have the same sort of  recognition that consistency and  robustness have  some work that studies what we would  call gracefulness uh we'll end up  calling it robustness so i think it's  important to kind of distinguish between  gracefulness as being a different thing  from robustness  and so that's something we're trying to  do in our work  okay so  we can uh where you can hope to achieve  consistency and gracefulness  and that's exactly what we're able to do  so our main contribution  is introducing a scheduling policy  that's the first that is a that has been  proven to be both consistent and  graceful in this stochastic scheduling  setting so in terms of gracefulness we  have constant g equals 3.5 and for  consistency c equals one so it's perfect  consistency and so what's new here is  both the policy itself  and these proofs uh that it's graceful  and consistent  and one thing i want to point out about  consistency in particular is that this  consistency result with c equals one is  impossible to achieve in the worst case  and so this is one difference between  the stochastic setting and the worst  case setting  all right so  let's uh recap this contribution we  introduced the first scheduling policy  that is both consistent and graceful  and so in the rest of this talk i'm  going to dissect this contribution so  the last question is like what is this  new policy and how do we bound its  performance but before we get there i  want to say a few words about what  policy even means  and  so there's a vast design space of  possible scheduling policies  and one thing we did to help ourselves  out was to narrow our focus to subclass  of scheduling policies still a very  broad class of scheduling policies but  something that was a bit easier to think  about  and and for which some queuing theory  existed to kind of help us evaluate  scheduling policies and i'll get to a  bit of that later so the design space we  ended up looking at was something called  uh scheduling policies that can be  described as rank functions  so  remember that a job's age is the amount  of time it's been in service so far  it turns out that lots of policies in  the scheduling literature can be  described as a job having some priority  which we'll call a rank  that varies as a function of age  so you know so we're going to say that  rank is some numerical priority lower  number means better priority the  server's always going to serve whichever  job has the lowest rank  and this rank can vary as a function of  a job's age and also perhaps of its  static characteristics in our case  that's going to mean size and estimated  size as an example srpt  fits into this framework it has the rank  function s minus a that is a job's rank  is its remaining size and so srpt always  serves the job of least remaining size  now of course we're going to try and  design a rank function that doesn't use  s right because we don't have access to  the true sizes so if we don't have s but  we do have z what might we do instead of  srpt well the obvious idea is well z is  going to be close enough to s so let's  just use z instead so this is a policy  that we call naive it's the naive  translation of srpt  into the size estimate setting  okay and s and naive of course also has  a rank function it's just z minus a  and so in this work we're going to be  focusing on the design space  of policies that can be described as  rank functions and so when we're trying  to design a policy we're really asking  the question what's the right rank  function  so let's start by looking at naive again  is naive the right rank function is it  going to be consistent and robust  and we might hope that naive performs  well because  z is close enough to s that z minus a is  close enough to s minus a and the  scheduling policy should do basically  the same thing  but unfortunately it turns out naive is  not even consistent  and the big problem with naive is that  its rank can go negative  so  what happens when a job has negative  rank well every job arriving into the  system  has positive rank and this job that just  went negative has the lowest rank in the  system because it's the one in service  and so no other job is going to catch it  it's going to be non-preemptable for the  rest of its time in the system  and because we have multiplicative  estimation error a really big job  that gets underestimated by even a small  factor could spend a really long time  with negative rank of being  non-preemptable and that can be really  bad news  for our mean response time  and so naive is not even  consistent all right so what can we do  instead how can we fix this problem of  naive well  i said the rank function going negative  was a problem what if we just didn't do  that right so this leads to a policy  called check mark  and check mark is consistent  but unfortunately check mark is not  graceful  at least not when beta is less than one  half  so what goes wrong when beta is less  than one half  well what does first let's remember what  beta less than one half means that means  that a job's estimated size  can be less than one half of its true  size so in the picture that would look  like a jobs true size being all the way  out here  and so let's think about what will  happen for this job uh with this true  size s and this much smaller estimated  size z  over the course of its uh service under  check mark so  by the time a job's reaching the end of  its uh time in service  its rank will have exceeded its initial  rank  and this is bad news because whenever  you reach a new worst rank of your time  so far in the system  that's an invitation for other jobs that  have been waiting with rank that was  higher than you  you might have just reached their rank  and so those jobs can now preempt you  and so whenever you reach a new worst  rank preemption becomes likely and so  checkmark has this tendency of  preempting jobs just as they're about to  finish  and so checkmark is not graceful  okay i'm out of room in my gray box of  policies that won't work so we have to  come up with the right one this time how  are we going to fix the problem with  check mark well i told you that the  problem with check mark was all about  exceeding this initial rank so what if  we just didn't do that so this leads to  a policy called radical  radical doesn't go negative  and doesn't exceed uh initial the  initial rank of a job  and we call the policy radical both  because it vaguely looks like a square  root sign  and because it has radically good  performance properties it is the one  consistent 3.5 graceful policy we've  been looking for  and so in the rest of this talk i'm  going to explain how we compare radicals  performance to srpt's performance which  is at the core of how we prove  our consistency and gracefulness results  unfortunately it turns out to be pretty  hard to directly compare radical to srpt  it's because even though their rank  functions kind of happen to coincide  when there's low noise um if there's  sorry when there's no noise if there's  even a little bit of noise then the fact  that radical starts at z and um  starts at z and then goes down to z uh  turns out to be different enough from  starting at s and going down to s  um that it's hard to do this direct  comparison  and so what we did is we  did this comparison by way of a third  policy called scale which is sort of  part way between radicals rank function  and srpt's rank function so like radical  a job's initial rank is z but like srpt  a jobs rank scales linearly down and  ends at zero by the time age equals size  s  um and so  what this means is that we can't  actually implement scale without knowing  a job's true size so you wouldn't really  use the scale policy in practice but  it's helpful for analysis because we can  compare radical to scale and then  compare scale to srpt  okay so how are we going to do these  comparisons so  uh this is where the second sentence of  this talk comes in which was that i'm in  the last year of my phd so the thesis  i'm writing is all about how do we  analyze scheduling policies in  stochastic queuing systems  and my thesis is all about two new tools  that i've developed  along with my collaborators including  isaac grossof and these new tools help  us analyze policies like radical scale  and srbt and in fact i'm really excited  about this paper in particular because  for the first time we had to use both  tools  so i'm going to tell you a little bit  about each of them so the first tool is  soap which we use to compare radical to  scale  and the second tool is called wine which  we use to compare scale to srbt so i'm  going to say just a few words about soak  and then i'll go a bit more depth into  wine and the reason i'm going to focus a  bit more on wine today is because wine  is actually potentially useful even for  worst case scheduling questions and so i  think that's a really important one to  share with the itcs community  okay so let's start with how we compare  radical to scale using this tool soap  so soap stands for schedule ordered by  hbase priority and what soap does is it  takes  basically any stochastic arrival process  and any rank function  and yields the response time  distribution it's a sort of complicated  formula the  formula for mean response time is a  little bit simpler but still pretty  complicated um  and so i'm going to leave defer the  details to the paper but the basic  version is that  because the radical and scale rank  functions are close enough to each other  and in particular because they both have  initial rank z  and never exceed that initial xe  the policies are close enough that soap  yields similar enough mean response  times for them  so that's how we do that first  comparison let's now talk about  comparing scale to srbt  using wine so wine stands for work  integral number equality and so unlike  soap which directly gave us response  time wine  is a way to get the number of jobs in  the system but this is fine because  there's a famous result in queuing  theory called little's law which says  that the mean response time is directly  proportional to the mean number of jobs  for any fixed arrival rate  and so  what and so the way wine gets us the  number of jobs in the system  is via this other quantity called our  work which i'll define soon and so what  mine does is it reduces the problem of  analyzing mean response time to the  problem of analyzing artwork or in our  case comparing scales artwork to srpt's  artwork so  in the rest of my talk i'm going to  answer some questions about wine first  what is our work second how does wine  give us n the number of jobs from our  work and then finally how do we use this  to bound scales mean response time  so let's start by defining our work and  before i define artwork let me just  define work without the r so when  queuing theorists talk about work they  just mean the  the total remaining size of all jobs in  the system  our work is going to be a filtered out  version of this total work so let's let  r be some  threshold  our work is the total remaining size of  all jobs  where i'm only counting the jobs that  are r and smaller in terms of their  remaining size so i like to visualize  our work as follows i've got some  threshold r glasses let me align the  jobs by remaining size and so when i put  on these glasses they're going to hide  the jobs that have remaining size above  threshold are  if i look at all the jobs that i can see  through the glasses and i add up their  remaining sizes  that's our work  and so the importance of our work is  that wine tells us how to get the number  of jobs from an r-work integral because  remember y stands for work integral  number equality  so wine says that under any scheduling  policy we get number of jobs from this  artwork integral  and so what we did is we basically  bounded scales our work in terms of  srpt's artwork here's what we showed we  showed specifically that scales artwork  was less than or equal to srpts mean  alpha over beta r work  the intuition here is that scale is  always serving a job not necessarily of  least remaining size but within an alpha  over beta factor of least remaining size  and so the fact that scale is only  making errors  by a factor of alpha over beta  means that its r work can be compared to  s rpt's alpha over beta artwork so we  use that same slack factor of alpha over  beta  um and so that's kind of that's kind of  the vague intuition for why this level  is true um and so once you have this  lemma and you have wine and you have  little's law you put them together and  you get this theorem that says uh and  this is a pretty simple computation once  you have the lemma um and this says that  mean response time of scale is at most  alpha over beta times mean response time  of srbt  so that's how we use wine to compare  scale to srbt so  a few more comments about things on this  slide first i want to mention that wine  also holds in the worst case in fact it  holds very generally it  the theorem is really that if you look  at any system  it doesn't matter kind of how the system  got to its current state if you just  take a picture of its current state and  you count the number of jobs and then  you do the r work integral you get the  same thing  so that's the first thing i want to say  the second thing i want to mention is  that this lemma and theorem can be  generalized slightly so  remember that i mentioned earlier that  scale isn't a policy you could actually  do because you have to know a job's true  size in order to scale linearly down to  it  but if you were in a system where you  had uh not just an initial size estimate  but a  dynamically updating remaining size  estimate  then you probably couldn't do exactly  scale but you might have a sort of noisy  version of scale that you could do  and this lemma will also work if you're  doing some sort of noisy version of  scale  so that's what i wanted to say about  wine in summary what wine does is it  reduces the mean response time  comparison to an artwork comparison  and we showed that the artwork amounts  were close enough between scale and srbt  that they have similar enough mean  response times  okay let's summarize what we talked  about today so we discussed the problem  of minimizing mean response time when we  only have access to noisy size estimates  and this is a difficult problem because  the natural policies we'd think of end  up performing badly for example the  naive translation of srpt into size  estimate world ends up not even being  consistent so it has poor performance  even when the noise is very low  so to solve this problem we came up with  a new policy which has provably good  performance specifically we showed it as  one consistent so perfect consistency  and 3.5 graceful  and we proved this result using a  creative cocktail of two recently  developed uh tools from cueing theory  and so i'm excited about this general  direction  of algorithms with predictions uh where  we kind of consider stochastic versions  of the problems and i'm interested in  developing tools uh theoretical tools  for analyzing these stochastic problems  that go beyond just scheduling and  queuing so if you're curious to talk  about algorithms with predictions or you  have any questions about this talk feel  free to get in touch i'd be delighted to  hear from you
 

